# Who has to review a change to the things IAM trusts.
#
# Write access to this repository is merge access to five workflow files pinned by name in
# three IAM trust policies -- and to the source and configuration that ship inside the two
# released Lambda zips. tools/build_admission_lambda.py copies config/*.yaml and the whole
# src/edullm_platform tree into the validator, so a change to either decides whether a run
# is authorized. That makes them the same kind of path as a workflow file rather than a
# different one, and they were not covered here.
#
# Extended when write access was first granted to somebody who did not build this platform.
# Until then, ownership covered the workflows and the infrastructure and left the admission
# validator's own source and the policy it enforces uncovered, so a change to the
# authorization rules needed one approving review from anybody holding write.
#
# CONFIG IS OWNED MORE WIDELY THAN THE REST, AND ONLY CONFIG. 84 of the 99 first-parent
# commits on main ending at b73cdd7 touched a path owned here and 29 of them touched config,
# counted on 2026-08-04. The window is anchored to a commit rather than left as "the last
# 99", which slides: measured the same way on 2026-08-05 it reads 85 and 23, so the sentence
# would have gone on asserting two numbers nobody had measured. The proportion is what the
# argument rests on and it has held. Branch protection sets
# require_code_owner_reviews with required_approving_review_count 1, and all 36
# collaborators already hold write or better. So the review queue for eight research teams
# was two people, and the largest single thing they were queueing on was config: profiles,
# workloads, the roster, datasets.
#
# config/ is also the least dangerous place to be wrong. Every file under it is loaded and
# cross-checked by the suite, which catches an incoherent profile, an unplaceable workload
# or a roster naming somebody who is not in the organization inside ninety seconds -- so a
# bad config change is caught by a machine before a reviewer would reach it. A trust policy
# or a workflow file is not like that: the failure is a run that cannot assume a role, in
# an account no test here can see. Those stay with the two.
#
# THE NINE LOGINS ARE SPELLED OUT RATHER THAN NAMED AS @edu-llm/team-leads, on purpose, and
# nine is `admins` plus `team_leads` in config/organization.yaml rather than the eight in
# `team_leads`. That is the set holds_routine_approver_role admits, so the people who may
# review a config change are the people who may release a routine run.
# test_config_is_owned_by_the_admins_and_exactly_the_recorded_team_leads derives this line
# from the roster and fails on a name added to one and not the other.
#
# The reason for logins rather than the team is not that the two agree today. It is that a
# team's membership is edited in organization settings and appears in no commit, so naming
# the team would hand ownership of config to whoever an owner adds, silently. Two earlier
# versions of this comment asserted the two agreed, dated the check, and were used to
# justify the arrangement. Both were false when read: the GitHub team gained a ninth member
# some time after 2026-07-31 and no hand-check caught it.
#
# That incident is the argument for spelling the logins out rather than against it. Nobody
# committed anything, and had this line said @edu-llm/team-leads the right to review config
# would have moved with it. As it happens the ninth member is BritishAmericqn, already named
# below as an admin, so this line did not move. The next one need not be an admin.
#
# What compares the two is not a comment. fixtures/evidence/phase-2/github/lead-team
# .sanitized.json is a committed capture of the GitHub team, checked against the roster in
# both directions by tests/test_phase2_github_evidence.py and expiring after thirty days.
# config/organization.yaml's header says how to re-take it and why no scheduled job can.
